  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\nWe will use a dual-phase optimization strategy combining two methods:\n- **Piecewise Domain Allocation:** We represent the step function h as a sequence of impulses or segments distributed across the domain [0,2]. This captures the essence of Erd\u0151s' idea of minimizing self-overlap through careful allocation of function values.\n- **Iterative Water-Filling:** This method methodically fills the domain's \"containers\" (considered as discrete bins) to minimize maximum overlap, drawing inspiration from information theory's water pouring algorithm.\n\nThe approach partitions the domain into `n_bins` segments, mapping continuous shifts to discrete indices, which enables more efficient optimization. We initialize with the parent solution and iteratively improve it by adjusting segment allocations. The method avoids the expensive full correlation computations during optimization iterations by using piecewise-defined function representations.\n\nThis differs from previous gradient-based or evolutionary approaches by:\n- Working directly with piecewise representations\n- Handling domain shifts more naturally\n- Potentially reducing the correlation computations to O(m) per evaluation instead of O(n\u00b2)\n\nLimitations include:\n- Requires careful parameter selection for the bin count and segment allocation\n- Assumes discrete representations sufficiently approximate continuous case\n- Might need resolution-specific tuning\n\nThe method aims for a \u223c0.0001 improvement (achieving 0.3808\u20130.3807) against the current 0.3808 benchmark.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\n\nVariables:\n- \\(n_pts\\): Grid size (parent resolution 384)\n- \\(dx = 2.0/n_pts\\)\n- \\(Z_{n_pts} = \\{0, 1\\}^{n_bins}\\): Discrete allocation decisions\n- \\(h_{vec}(i) \\equiv \\alpha \\prod_{k=1}^{bin(i)-1} \\sum_{j \\in bin_k} (1-\\alpha)\\): Cubic B-spline approximation of step indicator\n- \\(but we adapter mode \"h[i] = \\alpha if i \\in hence_candidate segments\"\n\nObjective/Constraint wrappings:\n- \\(target = n_pts/2\\)\n- \\(dx_2 = 2.0/n_bins\\): Coarse resolution for allocation\n- \\(\\alpha = (1 - (n_zeros)/n_nonzero) (energy conserving)\\)\n\nThe optimization seeks:\n   \\(\\min_{h} \\left( \\max_{k=-(n_bins-1)}^{(n_bins-1)} \\left| \\sum_{i=0}^{n_pts-1} h[i] \\cdot (1-h[i+k]) \\cdot dx \\right| \\right)\\)\n\nUsing transformed variables:\n   Let \\(a_j = 1\\) if bin j is fully active, \\(0\\) otherwise\n   \\(let zeros \\sim density from parent\\)\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\na. Set binary resolution: start with `n_bins = 32` or `64` (adjust by factor 2)\nb. Convert parent solution to discrete indices if available:\n   zig = parent / (history scaling)\n   counts = np.ceil(zig * smoothing)\nc. Set \\(\\alpha_{trial} = 0.9 * \\alpha + 0.1 * random trial\\)\nd. Compute initial \\(h_vec\\) via piecewise functions\ne. Evaluate C5 using full correlation but with reduced grid (half)\nf. Iterate:\n   while time < budget_s:\n      - Try `new_alpha = alpha + random_normal*step_size`\n      - Adjust for energy conservation\n      - Form new piecewise function\n      - Project to maintain sum\n      - Compute C5 with reduced computation\n      - Accept if improvement > tunable threshold\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations:\nDefault settings:\n   - n_bins = 64 (then cascade to finer 128)\n   - alpha_step = 0.02\n   - n_dims = 2^(ceil(log2(n_bins)))\n   - max_iter = 5000 (tunable by time)\n\nConditional triggers:\n   - If plateau (0.1s): reduce step_size x2\n   - If wall clock ~60% budget: switch to local refinement (coordinated descent)\n   - If C5 remains stationary: random population jump\n\nPhase-specific choices:\n   - Initialization: Use sawtooth wave as heuristic template\n   - Local search: Use tree search with least-angle changes to bins\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness:\nEstimation:\n- Base case n_pts=384, n_bins=32: ~1 eval per bin per shift\n- Python cost: Correlation mode='full' on reduced grid (8x faster than full)\n\nAdaptive implementation:\n   time_init = 0.5s\n   time_per_opt = estimate by:\n      time0 = time.time()\n      corr = np.correlate(h, 1-h, 'full')\n      ns = len(corr)-1\n      time1 = time.time()\n      t_cost = time1-time0 + cross_correlation with reduced grid\n\nProgressive strategy:\n   1. Start with coarsest bin resolution\n   2. Save best candidate per resolution\n   3. Cascade to finer bins upon success\n\nTotal budget capping:\n   if time > 0.9 * budget_s: return best_candidate\n   if C5 improvement < 1e-6 for 10 trials: increase bin resolution\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract:\nPost-optimizationChecks:\n   - Verify 0<=h[i]<=1 for all i via clipping projection\n   - Re-compute C5 with full resolution 384 to ensure2 have not refined enough\n   - Check n_samples = n_pts\n   - Ensure sum(h) * dx == 1 within 1e-6\n\nh_values\u8fd4\u56de must be numpy array of length n_pts\n\n\u6b64 describes a complete implementation specification within 150 lines. Proceed as follows:\n- use scipy, numpy, optimizations from scipy.optimize\n- define projection either via reusable:\n    def project_alpha(alpha, n_bins, n_p target_hist):\ndone\n\nfinal_ }",
